lesson: 69
title: Places in a line
stars: 1
explain: In a line, first, second, third… tell the place counted from one end.
explain: If you are 4th from the front and 6th from the back, count yourself only once: 4 + 6 - 1 = 9 people stand in the line.
explain: Without the -1 you would count yourself twice, once from each end.
vars: a=name p=1..15 q=1..15
need: p + q >= 4
answer: p + q - 1
exercise: {a} is {p.ordwords} from the front of a line and {q.ordwords} from the back. How many children are in the line?
exercise: In a race, {a} finished {p.ord}, and {a.he} was {q.ord} counting from the last runner. How many runners were in the race?
exercise: On a shelf, {a}'s book is the {p.ord} from the left and the {q.ord} from the right. How many books are on the shelf?
exercise: {a} stands in a queue. {a.He} is {p.ord} counting from the front and {q.ord} counting from the end. How many people are in the queue?
exercise: A row of houses has {a}'s house {p.ord} from one end and {q.ord} from the other end. How many houses are in the row?
test: Counting from the back, {a} is in place {q}; counting from the front, {a.he} is in place {p}. How many stand in the row?
test: A toy train pulls a row of cars. The red car is the {q.ordwords} car counting from the engine and the {p.ordwords} counting from the end. How many cars does the engine pull?
test: The race had 3 heats. On the final list {a} is {p.ord} from the top and {q.ord} from the bottom. How many children are on the list?
exercise: In a line for the school bus in Florida, {a} is {p.ord} from the front and {q.ord} from the back. How many children are in the line?
exercise: {a} is {q.ordwords} from the end of a row and {p.ordwords} from the start. How many are in the row?
exercise: How many people are in a queue if {a} is {p.ord} from the front and {q.ord} from the back?
exercise: At a bus stop in London, {a} counts that {a.he} is {p.ord} in the queue from the front and {q.ord} from the back. How many people are queuing?
exercise: In a row of beach huts in Brighton, {a}'s hut is {p.ord} from the left and {q.ord} from the right. How many huts are in the row?
exercise: In a class photo in Sydney, {a} stands {p.ordwords} from the left end and {q.ordwords} from the right end of the row. How many children are in that row?
exercise: {a} paddles in a line of canoes on a river in Canada: {p.ord} from the front, {q.ord} from the back. How many canoes are in the line?
exercise: On a list of names, {a}'s name is number {p} from the top and number {q} from the bottom. How many names are on the list?
test: A parade passes through Dublin. One float is {p.ord} counting from the front of the parade and {q.ord} counting from the rear. How many floats are in the parade?
test: In a stack of books in a Boston library, one book is {q.ord} from the top and {p.ord} from the bottom. How many books are in the stack?
test: How many cars stand in a traffic jam in Texas if {a}'s car is {p.ord} from the front and {q.ord} from the back?
test: Counting along a row of lockers from the door, {a}'s locker is number {p}; counting from the window end, it is number {q}. How many lockers are in the row?
test: In a relay team line-up, {a} is {q.ordwords} counted from the back and {p.ordwords} counted from the front. How many runners are in the line-up?
test: Ducks swim in a line on a pond in Wales. The white duck is {p.ord} from the front and {q.ord} from the back. How many ducks are in the line?
exercise: {a}'s {p.ord} from the front n {q.ord} from the back. how many in the line
exercise: race, {a} came {p.ord}, {q.ord} from last. how many runners
exercise: {a} is number {p+1} in the queue from the front, no sorry, number {p}, and number {q} from the back. how many in the queue
exercise: Kai says {p.ord} from front and {q.ord} from back means {p+q} kids. nah, whats right
test: book's {p.ord} from left, {q.ord} from right, shelf's like 2 metres long. how many books
test: {a} in the queue, {p} from front {q} from back, counting {a.him} both times. how many in the queue
test: cars in a jam, ours is {p.ord} from the front n {q.ord} from the back. how many cars
exam: Whereas {a} occupies the {p.ordwords} position from the front of a queue and the {q.ordwords} position from the rear thereof, determine the total number of persons in the queue.
exam: It is given that a competitor finished in {p.ord} place and, counted from the final finisher, in {q.ord} place. Ascertain the number of competitors in the race.
exam: State the number of books upon a shelf on which a certain book stands {p.ord} from the left and {q.ord} from the right.
exam: Compute the length of a single file of pupils in which {a} is reckoned {p.ordwords} from one end and {q.ordwords} from the other.
exercise: At the funeral, the mourners walked in a line behind the coffin. {a} was {p.ordwords} from the front and {q.ordwords} from the back. How many mourners were in the line?
exercise: {a}'s dad, who was laid off last week, is {p.ord} from the front of the queue at the job centre and {q.ord} from the back. How many people are in the queue?
test: bloody queue. {a}'s a bit economical with the truth — said {a.he} was there first, but {a.he}'s {p.ord} from the front and {q.ord} from the back. how many in it
